{"graph_chars": "The chapter-ending Bring It Home returns to information. Modern farmers use data rather than only older almanac methods. Information helps allocate scarce resources. If the media send ten reporters to cover one announcement, those reporters are not covering something else. Before you go on, the chapter tells you to read the math appendix. Here is the short recitation for Chapter One. Economics studies how humans make decisions in the face of scarcity. Scarcity is wants exceeding available supply. Time is the ultimate scarce resource. Division of labor splits production into tasks. Specialization raises output for three reasons: matching advantage, learning and quality, and economies of scale. Specialization requires trade. Micro looks at households, workers, and firms. Macro looks at growth, unemployment, and inflation. This course is macro. Monetary policy is the Federal Reserve. Fiscal policy is spending and taxes. The circular flow has households and firms in a product market and a labor market. Economies are traditional, command, market, or mixed. Globalization is trade plus financial capital flows. G D P measures total production. That is Chapter One.", "graph_times": [[0.0, 0.0628], [0.0628, 0.1256], [0.1256, 0.1884], [0.1884, 0.2512], [0.2512, 0.314], [0.314, 0.3768], [0.3768, 0.4396], [0.4396, 0.5024], [0.5024, 0.5652], [0.5652, 0.628], [0.628, 0.6908], [0.6908, 0.7536], [0.7536, 0.8164], [0.8164, 0.8792], [0.8792, 0.942], [0.942, 1.0048], [1.0048, 1.0676], [1.0676, 1.1304], [1.1304, 1.1932], [1.1932, 1.256], [1.256, 1.3189], [1.3189, 1.3817], [1.3817, 1.4445], [1.4445, 1.5073], [1.5073, 1.5701], [1.5701, 1.6329], [1.6329, 1.6957], [1.6957, 1.7585], [1.7585, 1.8213], [1.8213, 1.8841], [1.8841, 1.9469], [1.9469, 2.0097], [2.0097, 2.0725], [2.0725, 2.1353], [2.1353, 2.1981], [2.1981, 2.2609], [2.2609, 2.3237], [2.3237, 2.3865], [2.3865, 2.4493], [2.4493, 2.5121], [2.5121, 2.5749], [2.5749, 2.6377], [2.6377, 2.7005], [2.7005, 2.7633], [2.7633, 